     if i < N and A[i+1][j] > A[i][j]: dp[i][j] = max(dp[i][j], 1 + dp[i+1][j])
     if j < M and A[i][j+1] > A[i][j]: dp[i][j] = max(dp[i][j], 1 + dp[i][j+1])
3) Answer be dp[1][1].

Complexity: O(N*M) time and O(N*M) space per testcase — well within limits (N,M ≤ 100).

Tiny Python sketch:

def longest_from_start(A):
    N, M = len(A), len(A[0])
    dp = [[1]*M for _ in range(N)]
    for i in range(N-1, -1, -1):
        for j in range(M-1, -1, -1):
            if i+1 < N and A[i+1][j] > A[i][j]:
                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i][j], 1 + dp[i+1][j])
            if j+1 < M and A[i][j+1] > A[i][j]:
                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i][j], 1 + dp[i][j+1])
    return dp[0][0]

